+# Agent Note: Provider request message projection
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-27-request-layout-projection.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+`agent/pre-step` runs before entered messages become durable, while `agent/request` owns only call configuration. Neither event sees the complete Session-derived messages after current input and tool results join history. A plugin that needs a different Provider message order would otherwise rebuild the request inside an LLM adapter, separating the request header, invariant observation, and actual Provider input.
+
+The Session reconstruction invariant also compared the final request messages directly with `deriveMessages()`. That comparison correctly rejected unlogged content but could not distinguish a validated projection from corruption of its durable source boundary.
+
+## Decision
+
+`dsh-agent` declares the scoped `agent/request-layout` waterfall. Every payload carries the Agent and Session identity, turn, step, a one-based attempt that resets on each new step, effective call config, system text, tools, the complete frozen `messages` boundary, and the turn signal. The default result returns that `messages` array; listeners can replace only the array and use ordinary waterfall composition through `next()`.
+
+AgentLoop dispatches the waterfall after config and adapter-default resolution but before `request/header`, request-context logging, final freezing, and Provider dispatch. Returning the exact source array takes an identity path that reuses the already validated, deep-frozen boundary. A replacement is read once, cloned once into the only detached snapshot, and then validated and deep-frozen without consulting listener-owned values again. Validation checks each core content-block shape, conservatively permits plain extension blocks with a non-empty type, and tracks only pending top-level tool calls while checking result order. A result closes and removes its pending id, so a later settled span may reuse that opaque id; duplicates within one open span, interleaving, and unmatched results remain invalid. Invalid output ends the turn before a header or Provider call. The snapshot is frozen before any synchronous Session event, so a listener or event observer cannot change Provider input after validation.
+
+A package-private WeakMap associates each live loop request with its frozen Session-derived source boundary and whether the waterfall returned another array. The request invariant always reconstructs the source boundary from the live Session and still reconstructs config, system text, and tools from the folded header. Identity requests reuse that exact source boundary; transformed requests are accepted only with loop-recorded provenance and a frozen final messages array. The WeakMap is diagnostic process state, not durable history; neither the projection nor a complete request is written as a Session event.
+
+A plugin whose projection depends on another durable source owns that source, its request snapshot, and recovery validation. The core event and provenance contain no plugin domain fields.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Project during `agent/pre-step`.** Rejected because current claimed messages are not yet in the Session-derived boundary and tool results from the preceding step are outside the claimed batch. A projection built there can silently omit the exact input that triggered the request.
+
+**Rebuild inside an LLM adapter.** Rejected because an adapter would send data different from the frozen request observed by `llm/stream`, request-header reconstruction, and diagnostics. The adapter also owns Provider translation rather than Agent history policy.
+
+**Write every final projection into the Session log.** Rejected because the Session owns local conversation history, while projection plugins may reconstruct from another durable source. Copying complete requests into Session events would duplicate potentially large content and introduce plugin provenance into the core log.
+
+**Allow mutable request middleware.** Rejected because a listener could change messages after validation or change config, tools, and system text without matching header facts. A result-only waterfall followed by deep freezing keeps the mutation point explicit.
+
+## Testing
+
+Agent-loop tests pin default identity, complete claimed and tool-result boundaries, message reordering, validation before Provider dispatch, detached freezing before synchronous Session observers, settled call-id reuse across steps, one-based retry attempts and step reset, transformed invariant acceptance, and source-boundary mismatch rejection. The existing Agent and Agent Loop suites retain ordinary tool-call/result, retry, cancellation, and resume coverage without a layout listener.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Plugins can project a complete request without copying the Agent Loop or replacing Provider adapters, and ordinary agents keep byte-equivalent messages, system text, and tools without another message clone or validation pass. Every Provider attempt still pays for the awaited waterfall; transformed attempts additionally pay for one snapshot clone and validation. Process-local provenance can diagnose live dispatch but cannot reconstruct a projection after restart; a plugin that needs durable audit or recovery must persist and verify its own inputs and output identity.
